{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_yt4u37npk5re3xloj5kuosbbe5prjgdqhvuvwwl2t4xdldc67w3a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Build is successful, only warnings (which is expected since `cargo build` doesn't include `#[test]` functions; the items they reference appear \"unused\"). All 13 tests pass under `cargo test`.\n\n## Summary\n\n**Phase 1 — Translation (`src/coroutine.rs`):**\nReplaced every `unimplemented!()` with a working coroutine library. Since the C version uses `ucontext` to save/restore CPU state on a shared stack, but the harness forbids `unsafe` and FFI in spirit, I implemented coroutine semantics using a 1‑OS‑thread‑per‑coroutine model with bidirectional `mpsc` channels:\n- `coroutine_open` / `coroutine_close` allocate and tear down `Schedule` (matching the C struct exactly: `nco=0`, `cap=DEFAULT_COROUTINE`, `running=-1`, all slots `None`, full `STACK_SIZE` buffer).\n- `coroutine_new` reproduces the C slot‑placement algorithm: when full it doubles capacity and returns the old `cap` as the new id; otherwise it scans `(i+nco) % cap` for the first empty slot.\n- `coroutine_resume` either spawns a fresh worker thread (READY → RUNNING) or signals an existing one (SUSPEND → RUNNING) and blocks until the coroutine yields or finishes. On finish, it clears the slot, decrements `nco`, and resets `running=-1`, matching `mainfunc` in C.\n- `coroutine_yield` flips status to `SUSPEND`, sets `running=-1`, and parks the worker on its resume channel.\n- `coroutine_status` and `coroutine_running` mirror C exactly (DEAD for empty slots, current `running` field).\n\n**Phase 2 — Tests (`src/bin/test_coroutine.rs`):**\nWrote 13 `#[test]` functions covering every public symbol, with expected values derived from running the compiled C program (`c_src/main` / `tests/test.c` produces the canonical \"main start / coroutine 0 : 0 / coroutine 1 : 100 / …\" sequence which is asserted exactly). Edge cases covered: constants, freshly‑opened state, sequential id assignment, READY status, DEAD for empty slots, resume‑then‑suspend transitions, full lifecycle parity with `tests/test.c`, no‑yield completion, no‑op resume of a dead id, slot reuse after completion, capacity doubling at the boundary, and `coroutine_running` reporting the current id from inside the coroutine. Tests that share global state are serialized with a `TEST_LOCK`.\n\nAll 13 tests pass under `cargo test`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":354,"cache_read_input_tokens":41391,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":354,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b12da774-1daa-427c-a4e5-19fcf87a0b99","uuid":"bb086d43-5da3-447d-85d1-48edaf94386c"}
